"In the mountains, the land is steep and the water runs fast. Fast water is strong. It can roll gravel and even large rocks along the bottom, and these scrape the riverbed like tools. Little by little, the river cuts down into the rock. Over a very long time, this makes a deep valley.": "b8aad5ea0ced",
"Pick up a stone from a riverbed and look at it. It is smooth and round. It did not start that way. Once it was a sharp piece of a mountain, far upstream. The river broke it off, rolled it along, and rubbed its corners away. Every river is doing this, all the time.": "383ec567ba71",
"Pick up a stone from a riverbed and look at it. It is smooth and round. It did not start that way. Once it was a sharp piece of a mountain, far upstream. The river broke it off, rolled it along, and rubbed its corners away. Every river is doing this, all the time. A river has three jobs. First, it wears away the land. This is called erosion. Second, it carries the pieces downstream. Third, it puts them down again somewhere else. Which job it does depends on one thing: how fast the water is moving. In the mountains, the land is steep and the water runs fast. Fast water is strong. It can roll gravel and even large rocks along the bottom, and these scrape the riverbed like tools. Little by little, the river cuts down into the rock. Over a very long time, this makes a deep valley. Then the river leaves the mountains. The land becomes flatter, the river spreads out, and the water slows down. Slow water cannot carry heavy things, so the river drops the gravel right there, at the foot of the mountains. The pile it builds is shaped like an open fan, so it is called an alluvial fan. The lighter grains keep going. Sand is carried farther and settles across the lowland. Each flood spreads a new, thin layer, and after thousands of floods the layers add up to a wide, flat plain. The finest mud stays in the water the longest. Much of it reaches the sea, where the river finally stops moving, and settles there. Taiwan shows all of this in a short distance. The mountains are high and steep, the rain is heavy, and the sea is close, so the rivers are short and fast. The Zhuoshui River, the longest in Taiwan, carries so much sand and mud that its water is cloudy all year. Its name means muddy water river. The wide farmland on both sides of its lower course is made of what the river brought down from the mountains.": "767f2ce4203f",
"Taiwan shows all of this in a short distance. The mountains are high and steep, the rain is heavy, and the sea is close, so the rivers are short and fast. The Zhuoshui River, the longest in Taiwan, carries so much sand and mud that its water is cloudy all year. Its name means muddy water river. The wide farmland on both sides of its lower course is made of what the river brought down from the mountains.": "ce6e2f88a517",
